# Container image for The Pleiades controller: the API gateway, the
# scheduler lease holder, and the job dispatcher (cmd/controller).
#
# Two stages. The builder compiles with cgo against glibc; the runtime is
# a distroless glibc image with no shell and no package manager.
# Dockerfile.runner is deliberately the same file with a different binary
# and no published port, so a change to one belongs in the other too. The
# one place the two differ on purpose is the writable directory: this
# image has /data because this binary has relative-path defaults that need
# somewhere to land, and the runner image has none because nothing on the
# runner's path writes to its own filesystem.

# Debian bookworm, not alpine, and the choice is forced rather than
# stylistic. cmd/controller links github.com/mattn/go-sqlite3, which is a
# cgo driver, so the binary is dynamically linked against the C library it
# was built with. Alpine is musl and the runtime image below is glibc, so
# a musl-linked binary cannot start there at all. Turning cgo off to dodge
# that is the trap explained at the build step.
#
# Pinned by digest as well as by tag. A tag is a moving pointer that can
# name a different image tomorrow; only the digest names one exact image,
# so two people building on different days get the same compiler. This is
# the stronger form of the rule docker-compose.yml already follows by
# pinning exact versions.
FROM golang:1.26-bookworm@sha256:116d58cbd88c1297624acc6e967a060012422bacf9930927e23fb719189c6f36 AS builder

WORKDIR /src

# The module definition on its own, ahead of the source, so that the
# dependency download lands in a layer that editing a .go file does not
# invalidate.
COPY go.mod go.sum ./
RUN go mod download

COPY . .

# The final image's directory tree is assembled here, in the one stage
# that still has a shell. The runtime image below has none: no RUN, no
# mkdir, no chown can ever run there, so anything the running container
# needs has to exist before the COPYs that carry it across.
#
# Two roots, because they get two different owners in the final image and
# a single COPY can only carry one --chown (see the COPYs below for why
# that split is the point):
#
#   /staging/app   the binary and its read-only data, root-owned there
#   /staging/data  the one directory the running process may write to
#
# RUNBOOK_DIR is resolved at startup and the controller exits if the
# directory is missing, so the image has to contain one. It only ever gets
# read (internal/runbook.NewDirSource stats, lists and reads; it creates
# nothing), so it belongs on the read-only side. A deployment mounts its
# real runbooks over this path.
RUN mkdir -p /staging/app/runbooks /staging/data

# Built as a PACKAGE path, not a single file. A single-file build
# silently stops compiling the moment the composition root is split
# across more than one file, while `make ci` (go build ./...) stays
# green, so the image breaks and nothing else does.
#
# CGO_ENABLED=1 is set explicitly because the wrong value here fails in
# the worst possible way. With cgo off the build still succeeds, and
# go-sqlite3 links a stub that only fails when a query runs, so the
# controller dies inside its first schema migration on its own default
# DB_DSN of sqlite://controller.db. That is a green build that breaks for
# every operator who does not override the database setting, and it is
# exactly what the previous alpine builder shipped: the official
# golang:*-alpine images set CGO_ENABLED=0 for you, because they carry no
# C compiler.
#
# -trimpath strips build-machine filesystem paths out of the binary, so
# the same source produces the same output regardless of where it was
# built. -s -w drop the symbol table and the DWARF debug info, which are
# a large share of the binary's size and are of no use in a production
# container. Neither flag changes behavior.
#
# What this build cannot do is stamp the commit. `go build` reads
# vcs.revision out of .git, and .dockerignore keeps .git out of the build
# context on purpose: it is a 60 MB non-input, and the deny-by-default
# shape that excludes it is the same control that keeps .SPECIFICATION
# out. So the commit travels in as a build argument instead, into an OCI
# label (see the label block in the runtime stage) and into the binary
# (below).
#
# One consequence worth knowing: cgo means this stage cannot cross
# compile. It builds for the architecture it runs on.
# VERSION and VCS_REF also reach the binary itself, through
# internal/buildinfo, so `controller version` and the external Collection
# loader know which build this is. The binary decides what they mean: a
# VERSION that is not a release (the default "unknown", a branch name)
# makes it a development build, 0.0.0-dev+<VCS_REF>. Both are checked to
# hold only characters a version or a commit can, since they are spliced
# into the linker's flags and a space would start a flag of its own.
ARG VERSION=unknown
ARG VCS_REF=unknown
RUN case "$VERSION$VCS_REF" in *[!A-Za-z0-9.+-]*) echo "VERSION and VCS_REF may hold only letters, digits, '.', '+' and '-'" >&2; exit 1;; esac; \
    CGO_ENABLED=1 go build -trimpath \
      -ldflags="-s -w -X github.com/Subject-Void-LLC/the-pleiades/internal/buildinfo.version=$VERSION -X github.com/Subject-Void-LLC/the-pleiades/internal/buildinfo.revision=$VCS_REF" \
      -o /staging/app/controller ./cmd/controller

# THE BACKUP IMAGE: the same controller binary beside PostgreSQL's own
# pg_dump and pg_restore, for `make backup`, `make restore` and `make decom`
# (docker-compose.yml's backup service builds this stage by name). It is
# not the default target, and nothing else starts from it: a build with no
# --target produces the distroless stage at the bottom of this file, which
# has no shell and no database tools, and which the controller runs as.
#
# PostgreSQL's official image, at the server's own release: pg_dump must be
# at least the server's major version, and matching the server exactly
# means the backup is written by the same code that will read it back. The
# tag is bookworm, not the alpine the server runs, because the binary above
# links glibc (Debian 12's, through golang:*-bookworm) and alpine has musl.
# internal/testsupport's TestBackupImageMatchesTheServer holds this
# version to the server's.
#
# Digest-pinned for the same reason the builder is.
FROM postgres:15.19-bookworm@sha256:5d1d70e254e3c5d7d76847a9deebb18478cd518df37abf6b278d4bdb1fe5d96c AS backup

COPY --from=builder /staging/app/controller /app/controller

# No entrypoint script: the image's own runs initdb logic this container
# must never run. The make targets pass the subcommand and --user.
ENTRYPOINT ["/app/controller"]

# gcr.io/distroless/base-debian12 holds glibc, CA certificates and
# tzdata, and essentially nothing else. There is no shell, no package
# manager and no busybox, so a process that escapes into this container
# finds no interactive foothold and nothing to pivot through.
#
# distroless/static and scratch were both rejected for one reason: they
# ship no libc, and the cgo SQLite driver linked above needs one. The
# :nonroot tag is the same image with its user already set to UID 65532,
# so the image cannot be run as root by accident.
#
# Digest-pinned for the same reason the builder is.
FROM gcr.io/distroless/base-debian12:nonroot@sha256:b12529fbbd0bb15eea8905f69d83148679e0b4d7d434c8808100792029b1caae

# Provenance, which this image carried none of before: no labels and, for
# the reason given at the build step, no Go VCS stamp either, so nothing
# in a built image said which commit produced it.
#
# Defaults are present so a bare `docker build` still succeeds and says
# plainly that it does not know, rather than failing or, worse, claiming a
# revision it never had. A build that wants real provenance passes them:
#
#   docker build -f Dockerfile.controller \
#     --build-arg VCS_REF="$(git rev-parse HEAD)" \
#     --build-arg VERSION="$(git describe --tags --always --dirty)" \
#     -t pleiades-controller:dev .
#
# Read them back with:
#
#   docker inspect -f '{{json .Config.Labels}}' pleiades-controller:dev
ARG VCS_REF=unknown
ARG VERSION=unknown
ARG SOURCE=https://github.com/Subject-Void-LLC/the-pleiades

# GPL-3.0-only is the SPDX identifier for the LICENSE file at the root of
# this repository. Nothing in this project grants the "or any later
# version" option, so the label must not claim it; if that ever changes,
# this string changes with it.
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="Pleiades Controller" \
      org.opencontainers.image.description="Pleiades control plane: API gateway, scheduler lease holder and job dispatcher." \
      org.opencontainers.image.licenses="GPL-3.0-only" \
      org.opencontainers.image.source="${SOURCE}" \
      org.opencontainers.image.revision="${VCS_REF}" \
      org.opencontainers.image.version="${VERSION}"

# Two COPYs, and the split between them is the entire ownership model.
#
# /app arrives root-owned and world readable and executable, which is the
# correction to what this image used to do. It used to copy the whole tree
# with --chown=nonroot:nonroot, so /app and /app/controller were mode 0755
# owned by 65532, the same uid the process runs as. A compromised process
# could not overwrite the running binary (the kernel refuses a write to a
# running executable's text), but it did not need to: it could unlink the
# file and write its own in place, and the next start would run that. That
# survives `docker compose restart`, `docker restart`, a restart policy
# and a host reboot, because it is the same container's writable layer, so
# one process compromise became a permanent one. Root-owned 0755 removes
# the class outright: 65532 may read and execute, and may not unlink.
COPY --from=builder /staging/app /app

# ...and the one directory that genuinely must be writable at run time,
# which is neither the binary nor the runbooks.
#
# It exists because two of this binary's defaults are relative paths:
# DB_DSN defaults to sqlite://controller.db and CONTROLLER_CREDENTIALS_DIR
# defaults to ".", both resolved against the working directory. An
# operator who overrides neither needs a writable working directory or the
# controller exits during its first schema migration.
#
# Numeric 65532:65532, not the nonroot:nonroot name that resolves to the
# same pair. The number is what the kernel checks, and it is what an
# operator has to match when they mount a volume here, so the two places
# that must agree are written the same way.
COPY --from=builder --chown=65532:65532 /staging/data /data

# The working directory is the writable one, so those relative defaults
# land somewhere the process can actually write.
#
# That moves the working directory off /app, which RUNBOOK_DIR's own
# default ("runbooks", also relative) was quietly depending on, so the
# absolute path is stated here instead. It is stated as RUNBOOK_DIR rather
# than as a baked DB_DSN deliberately: cmd/controller refuses DB_DSN and
# DB_PATH together on purpose, so an image that set DB_DSN would turn the
# documented SQLite shorthand `-e DB_PATH=/data/controller.db` into a
# startup error about a variable the operator never set.
WORKDIR /data
ENV RUNBOOK_DIR=/app/runbooks

# Numeric, never the name "nonroot", even though the base image defines
# that name and `USER nonroot:nonroot` reads better.
#
# Kubernetes cannot verify a non-numeric user. A pod with
# securityContext.runAsNonRoot: true fails to start against an image whose
# USER is a name, with CreateContainerConfigError and "image has
# non-numeric user (nonroot), cannot verify user is non-root". The kubelet
# has no /etc/passwd to resolve the name against, and refusing is the only
# safe answer available to it.
#
# This shipped broken and no gate here could see it: Compose has no
# runAsNonRoot concept at all, so the same image runs healthy under
# `docker compose up` while every hardened pod spec refuses it. It was
# found by really installing into a kind cluster, and reproduced on two
# Kubernetes versions with byte-identical kubelet text.
#
# 65532 is the uid and gid the distroless nonroot tag already uses, so this
# changes the spelling and not the identity.
USER 65532:65532

EXPOSE 8080

# Running with an immutable root filesystem, stated accurately because the
# comment that used to sit here was not. It said a deployment "points
# DB_DSN at a mounted volume and runs the container with a read-only root
# filesystem", and that command exits 1 during the first migration.
# --read-only was never the cause: the identical run without it failed the
# same way. A fresh Docker NAMED volume is created root-owned 0755 and the
# process is UID 65532, so it could not create the database file, and
# SQLite reports that as "unable to open database file: no such file or
# directory", which sends an operator hunting a missing path that is
# right there.
#
# What works, run against this image rather than reasoned about. Every
# flag here is load bearing, including the broker: this binary fails
# closed at startup on an unreachable NATS, so a command that omitted it
# would be one more comment describing a deployment that does not run.
#
#   docker volume create pleiades-data
#   docker run --read-only \
#     -v pleiades-data:/data \
#     --network <the network the broker is on> \
#     -e NATS_URL=nats://<broker>:4222 \
#     -e MASTER_ENCRYPTION_KEY=<base64 of 32 bytes> \
#     -e JWT_SECRET=<at least 32 bytes> \
#     pleiades-controller:dev
#
# No chown step, and that is because of the COPY above rather than by
# luck: Docker initializes an empty named volume from the image directory
# it is mounted over, including that directory's ownership, so a volume
# mounted at /data comes out owned by 65532 exactly because /data is. The
# default relative DSN then resolves inside the volume and the database
# survives the container.
#
# Two things that still need saying. A BIND mount (-v /host/path:/data) is
# NOT initialized from the image at all, so the host directory has to be
# chowned to 65532 first, and that is the case that still bites exactly
# the way the old comment described. And --read-only leaves /tmp read-only
# as well: the run above proves nothing on the startup or readiness path
# needs it, but docker-compose.yml mounts a tmpfs there anyway, which is
# the right habit for a Go process that may reach os.TempDir down a path
# nobody has exercised yet.

# There is deliberately no HEALTHCHECK instruction, and the reason has
# changed, so the old one is worth correcting rather than deleting. It
# used to say a healthcheck "would need an HTTP client binary this image
# deliberately does not ship". That is no longer true: cmd/controller
# grew a `healthcheck` subcommand (cmd/controller/healthcheck.go) that
# asks its own /readyz on LISTEN_ADDR and exits 0 only when the controller
# reports ready, so the one executable in this image can now probe itself.
#
# What has not changed is who owns the schedule. Interval, timeout,
# retries and start period are deployment decisions, and a Kubernetes
# probe ignores a baked HEALTHCHECK entirely, so the instruction would
# only ever describe the docker-compose case. docker-compose.yml declares
# it there:
#
#   test: ["CMD", "/app/controller", "healthcheck"]
#
# and any `docker run` that wants the same can pass --health-cmd. An
# orchestrator probing from OUTSIDE the container should still point
# straight at /readyz and needs nothing from the image.

CMD ["/app/controller"]
